finding: Re-review of HEAD 94c50e4a: finding. The unconditional opaque-entity CPU-history tax is repaired, and Orbit now visually follows a corrected target, but the Orbit fix violates fixed/presentation isolation and automatic enrollment has no retirement path. 1. VisualCorrection now reaches GlobalTransform and fixed through Orbit (src/extras/orbit/follow.ts:40-44; src/extras/orbit/index.ts:447-460). followPosition returns the corrected rendered target pose, then OrbitSystem writes it into the camera's authored Transform. Derivation publishes that correction into camera GlobalTransform, which the next fixed system reads. Independent CPU-only composed probe: target Transform [3,4,5], correction [10,0,0], Orbit camera yaw/pitch 0, distance 6; after step(0), a fixed callback on world.tick() observed camera GlobalTransform.x=13 rather than the uncorrected 3. The new follow.test.ts assertion that camera GlobalTransform equals [13,4,11] actually blesses this leak. Stage 2 says the offset is never read by fixed, and the earlier review specifically required the follow repair to preserve that boundary. Keep the camera's correction in presentation placement instead of baking the corrected target into its authored/fixed pose. The original target's own GlobalTransform remains clean; that alone does not establish isolation for followers. 2. Automatic enrollment retains history for past readers indefinitely (src/extras/orbit/follow.ts:24-34,40-41; src/standard/rendering/material-type.ts:1207-1228; marker deletion only at src/core/rendering/global-transform.ts:125-137). Orbit adds RenderTransformHistory to each target but never releases it on retarget/removal; transparent enrollment similarly only adds and never retires on becoming opaque. Component requires also leave the marker after Camera/light/text/audio removal. Thus a single camera visiting a large persistent population eventually pays CPU history and fixed sample-copy work for all visited entities even though only one is followed. Independent CPU-only probe retargeting one Orbit camera across 20 live Transform entities retained 20 CPU histories, not a bounded current-camera/current-target set. The no-reader fixture tests only entities that have never had a reader, and the removal test explicitly removes the marker, so both miss this case. Engine-owned enrollment needs an engine-owned retirement/ownership policy that does not remove another active reader's request; the game should not have to track implicit requests from unrelated engine modules to reclaim them. Baseline-red claim CONFIRMED independently as requested, using git archive 15d34bbd | tar -x into /tmp/shallot-stage2-baseline.Pz5nJM (not a worktree), with node_modules symlinked to installed dependencies. Cold standalone bun test ./src/standard/rendering/entity-weight.node.ts failed at 817.129 bytes/entity against 780 on 15d34bbd. Same standalone command on new HEAD failed at 817.504; GPU instance data was 5.333 on both. The cold failure is therefore a baseline issue, not a new stage finding. I did not independently reproduce the full-selection warm memory number. Audit/public seam judgment: Camera and directional/point/spot lights require the marker; text, Sound and Listener require it; StandardRendering runs transparent enrollment ahead of history capture; Orbit pre-enrolls targets; StandardAudio and Orbit depend on the CPU-capable history plugin, and Rendering includes it. Normal pre-capture readers are enrolled and share corrected interpolation. Cold/new/late enrollment's current-pose fallback is documented. readRenderGlobalTransform remains the single interpolation/correction algorithm; composeRenderGlobalTransform is its useful matrix form. Advance enrollment is earned for custom CPU readers, since an internal first-read side effect cannot recover a previous sample, and a public marker plus CPU-only plugin lets a creator declare that need. The name states what is retained. Built-in readers should enroll internally as they now do, but the current public marker alone does not solve shared automatic-request lifetime (finding 2). Independently examined new diff, sparse CPU capture/read/recovery/membership, lazy correction-column binding, all changed built-in enrollment sites, Orbit follow and its simulation authoring path, public exports/contracts, memory and history fixtures. Ran correction GPU, CPU enrollment GPU, GPU history, transform recovery, light history, shared transparent readers, Orbit follow/geometric mover and audio: 65 pass across 9 files. Independent fixed-isolation and enrollment-retirement probes both failed as above, then were removed. Long allocation oracle not rerun. Native GPU adapter identity absent; no hardware qualification claimed.
